---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Competitive Analysis" description="A plugin for ChatGPT and Claude that turns 'competitive analysis of this company' into an evidence-graded teardown.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-16">
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Competitive Analysis</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-12">Evidence-graded teardowns, in ChatGPT and Claude.</p>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none
      prose-p:leading-relaxed prose-p:mb-6
      prose-a:text-blue-600 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline">

      <p>
        Ask a model to "research this company" and you get the marketing site, paraphrased. Every claim arrives with the same confidence whether it came from a filing or a launch tweet, nothing is dated, and the gaps go unmentioned because absence doesn't look like a finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Competitive Analysis is a plugin for ChatGPT and Claude that runs the teardown the way I'd want a strategy analyst to run it. Ask it for a "competitive analysis of <em>company</em>" and it comes back with a verdict first, then the handful of findings that decide it, and every material claim carrying a link, an evidence date, and one of three labels:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>VERIFIED</strong>: it read the primary source itself.</li>
        <li><strong>COMPANY-CLAIMED</strong>: the company said it, or press repeated the company. Funding announcements live here.</li>
        <li><strong>ESTIMATE</strong>: its own triangulation, with the method shown, or a headcount figure from a data tool you connected, with the tool named.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Four methods are required on every run, because that's where the alpha is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Positioning history</strong> from the Wayback Machine. What the homepage and pricing page used to say, and the day they changed.</li>
        <li><strong>Job-listing mining.</strong> What a company is actually building shows up in job descriptions months before it ships.</li>
        <li><strong>Contradictions</strong> between sources, including the company's own pages. A discrepancy is a finding, not noise.</li>
        <li><strong>Absence as evidence.</strong> The review site with no reviews. The blog posts that now 404.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It ends with one of four verdicts: compelling, promising but unproven, structurally challenged, or unclear. If it couldn't reach something, it says what and why, instead of filling the hole with prose.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">The server behind it</h2>
      <p>
        The first version was only instructions, and the instructions held up. What kept failing was getting to the evidence: the Wayback Machine refused connections partway through a run, a job board blocked the app's fetcher, and one model fetching twenty pages in a row left the dates fuzzy. So the plugin now has a small server I run, with four tools that do that work in one place:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Page history:</strong> when a page's copy changed, bracketed between two dated Wayback Machine captures.</li>
        <li><strong>Job openings:</strong> the company's open roles from its public Greenhouse, Lever or Ashby board.</li>
        <li><strong>Hacker News mentions:</strong> how much Hacker News talked about it, year by year.</li>
        <li><strong>SEC filings:</strong> what it has filed with the SEC, including Form D fundraising notices.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All four read free, public sources, need no sign-in and keep no personal data (the <a href="/competitive-analysis/privacy/">privacy page</a> has the details). If the server is busy or down, the teardown still finishes with the app's own browsing and tells you what it couldn't check.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Bring your own research tools, or don't</h2>
      <p>
        The plugin holds no keys, yours or anyone's. If you have <a href="https://www.clay.com/guides/how-to-use-clay-in-chatgpt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clay</a> connected, it spends a little of your Clay credit on two things: the company's headcount history and its latest funding round. It never looks up the people who work there. A team that shrank right through a pivot tells you a lot. Without Clay, it runs on built-in browsing and tells you where that left gaps. Reddit coverage needs a community connector; without one, the report says "Reddit not searched" rather than guessing at sentiment.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing I learned in testing: with a search plugin like Exa installed, ChatGPT sends every page fetch through it, and those fetches couldn't open the Wayback Machine or the job boards. Exa's search turned up more competitors, but the history and hiring checks came back empty, so I run this one without it. Those reads now go through the plugin's own server, so this only matters when the server is busy.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Where it came from</h2>
      <p>
        This plugin is the public version of the <a href="https://github.com/amr05008/agent-skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">competitive-analysis skill</a> I run in Claude Code. Mine does the same job with a few more moving parts (four parallel researchers, a paid Reddit gateway, a report that lands in a file). The plugin is the same method, written for a model that has a browser and whatever plugins you've connected, plus the four tools above. The source for the plugin and its server is at <a href="https://github.com/amr05008/competitive-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/amr05008/competitive-analysis</a>.
      </p>
      <h2 id="support" class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Support</h2>
      <p>
        Questions, a bug, or a teardown that missed something? Email me at <a href="mailto:aaron@aaronroy.com?subject=Competitive%20Analysis%20plugin">aaron@aaronroy.com</a> with the company you asked about and I'll take a look.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/competitive-analysis/privacy/">Privacy</a> · <a href="/competitive-analysis/terms/">Terms</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
